{"records":[{"id":"gbdfwg325y","title":"Bacteriostatic antibiotics promote CRISPR-Cas adaptive immunity against phages by enabling increased spacer acquisition","description":"Phages impose strong selection on bacteria to evolve resistance against viral predation. Bacteria can rapidly evolve phage resistance via receptor mutation, or using their CRISPR-Cas adaptive immune systems. Acquisition of CRISPR immunity relies on the insertion of a phage-derived sequence into CRISPR arrays in the bacterial genome. Using Pseudomonas aeruginosa and its phage DMS3vir as a model, we demonstrate that conditions that reduce bacterial growth rates, such as exposure to bacteriostatic antibiotics (which inhibit cell growth without killing), promote the evolution of CRISPR immunity. We demonstrate that this is due to slower phage development under those conditions, which provides more time for cells to acquire phage-derived sequences and mount an immune response. Our data reveal that the speed of phage development is a key determinant of the evolution of CRISPR immunity, and suggest that use of bacteriostatic antibiotics can trigger elevated levels of CRISPR immunity in human-associated and natural environments. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-04T13:32:56.091Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gbdfwg325y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gbdfwg325y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tatiana","last_name":"Dimitriu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Antibiotics","Bacteriophage","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5jf5vppk4","title":"Normal Cell Differentiation Potential of Cancer Stem Cells Without Reprogramming Pluripotent Factors: a Novel Strategy in Stem Cell-Based Therapy for Tissue Regeneration ","description":"     Stem cells hold great promise for tissue regeneration and have the potential to treat many incurable degenerative diseases. Cancer stem cells (CSCs), or cancer initiating cells, have the ability to self-renew and differentiate into heterogeneous lineages of cancer cells. Current stem cell therapies face limitations, such as limited stem cell sources, time consumption, tumor formation, and immune rejection upon allogeneic transplantation. Allogeneic stem cell treatments simplify stem cell manufacturing and reduce transplant time, but their therapeutic potential is limited by human leukocyte antigen (HLA)-matched donors. CSCs retain characteristics essential for tissue regeneration. However, several limitations hinder cancer stem cell reprogramming with pluripotent factors. The development of 3D culture models for tissue imitating extracellular matrix in cancer cell lines aims to enhance CSC enrichment. This mini-review focuses on a new strategy for treating incurable degenerative diseases involving in vitro and in vivo 3D cancer models and the induced differentiation of CSCs into mature normal cell types. This allows tissue survival without immune rejection and offers a safe alternative to cancer stem cell reprogramming with pluripotent factors. In conclusion, preservation and banking of allogeneic CSCs offer an alternative, readily available, and safe strategy that can be used to facilitate stem cell-based cell therapy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-01T06:32:29.797Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5jf5vppk4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5jf5vppk4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Josita Alloysius ","last_name":"Josita","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Banking","Stem Cells Research","Regenerative Medicine","Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell","Cancer Stem Cell","Degenerative Deformity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"js5w49wgmm","title":"nCounter immune profiling of RC17 hESC-derived neural progenitor cells (NPCs) and foetal ventral mesencephalon (fVM) dopamine cells ","description":"Here we performed targeted transcriptional analysis of immune relevant genes in RC17-human embyronic stem cells (hESCs), RC17-hESC derived neural progenitor cells (NPCs) and mature dopamine neurons (DAn), differentiated according to Nolbrant et al. 2017, with comparison to human primary foetal ventral mesencephalon. All cell types were analysed either without treatment (NT) or after 24hr exposure to 5 ng/ml interferon gamma (IFNg) stimulation. The RC17-hESC derived NPCs are currently being investigated in the STEM-PD clinical trial as a dopamine cell replacement for Parkinson's disease (NCT05635409), here we utilised the Nanostring nCounter Human Immunology V2 panel, plus an additional 20 gene codeset designed to capture additional genes related to T cell activation/inhibition and graft rejection, to measure changes in immune gene expression throughout the differentiation process from hESC to mature DAn and with comparison to previously engrafted, and well-tolerated, fVM tissue. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-08T13:36:11.423Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/js5w49wgmm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/js5w49wgmm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Annabel","last_name":"Curle","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shaline","last_name":"Fazal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoling","last_name":"He","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Roger","last_name":"Barker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joanne","last_name":"Jones","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05t17k830","name":"UK Regenerative Medicine Platform","grant_id":"MR/S020934/1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","grant_id":"G101558"}],"categories":["Immunology","Life Sciences","Cell Biology","Stem Cell Therapy","Dopaminergic Neuron","Cell Replacement Therapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6rfc2xrnyd","title":"Substantial somatic genomic variation and selection for BCOR mutations in human induced pluripotent stem cells ","description":"Human Induced Pluripotent Stem Cells (hiPSC) are an established patient-specific model system where opportunities are emerging for cell-based therapies. We compared and contrasted hiPSCs derived from different tissues in the same individual and across two large hiPSC cell repositories. We show extensive single-nucleotide mutagenesis in all hiPSC lines, although fibroblast-derived hiPSCs (F-hiPSCs) are particularly heavily mutagenized by ultraviolet (UV)-related damage. We analysed genome sequencing data on 454 F-hiPSCs, 101 blood-derived hiPSCs (B-hiPSCs) and 141 B-hiPSC derived subclones, as well as transcriptional data on 78 B-hiPSCs to gain further insights. Across 324 whole genome sequenced (WGS) F-hiPSCs derived by the Human Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell Initiative (HipSci), UV-related damage is present in ~72% of cell lines, sometimes resulting in  substantial mutagenesis (range 0.25-15 per Mb). Furthermore, we find remarkable genomic heterogeneity between independent F-hiPSC clones derived during the same round of reprogramming, due to oligoclonal populations within fibroblasts. B-hiPSCs showed lower levels of genome-wide mutations (range 0.28-1.4 per Mb), no UV damage, but a strikingly high prevalence of acquired BCOR mutations in ~25% of lines. Genome-wide selection analysis revealed strong selection pressure for BCOR mutations in F-iPSCs and B-iPSCs. Directed differentiation experiments and RNA sequencing showed that BCOR mutations have functional consequences in affected B-hiPSCs. All hiPSCs had otherwise stable, diploid genomes on karyotypic pre-screening. Our work strongly suggests detailed nucleotide-resolution characterization is required prior to using hiPSCs for disease modelling or clinical applications. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-08T09:59:59.455Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6rfc2xrnyd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6rfc2xrnyd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Serena","last_name":"Nik-Zainal","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell","DNA Mutation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jv32cvpn3r","title":"CAD5 Brie Library Quality Control","description":"Whole-genome mouse CRISPR-KO Brie library in CAD5 cells - quality control run.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-10T14:21:40.826Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jv32cvpn3r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jv32cvpn3r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Beauchemin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R01NS118796"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R01NS117276"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"1R37NS125431"}],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vtvxykv2cr","title":"2020_Cell reports resubmission tables","description":"All tables associated with our paper titled \"High throughput CRISPR screening identifies genes involved in macrophage viability and inflammatory pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-15T08:44:50.23Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vtvxykv2cr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vtvxykv2cr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Carpenter","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Macrophage","Immunity","Innate Immunity","Screening","Pooled Analysis","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129777,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":178,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129777,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":129253,"restricted":63,"embargoed":461},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2177},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1331},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":625},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":459},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":434},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":402},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":367},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":297},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":265}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":127174},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1897},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":62},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":38},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":871},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count